    "result_text": "I've implemented `answer()` in `app/chat.py` and added four tests to `tests/test_chat.py`. The full suite passes (6 tests). The tests use fake clients that raise the same errors openai does, so nothing has been tried against the real API.\n\n**How `answer()` behaves**\n- **Normal case:** it joins the output of `iter_answer` into one string. A retry starts the request over. If an attempt drops partway through, its partial text is thrown away, so only the attempt that finished is returned.\n- **What counts as a failure:** in openai 3.19.2, a connection that fails at the start and one that drops mid-stream both come out as `openai.APIConnectionError`. A stalled read comes out as `openai.APITimeoutError`. Both are raised from the underlying network error from the `httpx2` library.\n- **Timeouts are checked first:** `APITimeoutError` is a subclass of `APIConnectionError`, so the timeout case has to be caught before the connection case. It raises `UpstreamTimeout` immediately with no retry.\n- **Warning logs:** openai's own message is just \"Connection error.\", so each warning on `app.chat` logs the wrapped network error instead. It looks like `Attempt 1/3 to reach the model provider failed: RemoteProtocolError: peer closed connection without sending complete message body`.\n- **All attempts fail:** after 3 failures it raises `UpstreamUnavailable`, chained to the last error.\n- **History:** it's read into a list once at the start, so a one-shot iterator still works on retries.\n\n**Tests added:** a clean answer; a stream that drops and then succeeds, checking that no partial text leaks and that the network message appears in the warnings; failure on all 3 attempts; and a timeout that is not retried.\n\n**Retry counts multiply:** I used the client as given, so its own `max_retries` still applies inside each attempt when a connection fails before streaming starts. A failure there can reach the provider up to 3 \u00d7 (1 + `max_retries`) times before `UpstreamUnavailable` is raised. A drop mi"
